{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And Israel saw, etc.</b> It is difficult: were they not sitting before him for seventeen years studying Torah, and how does he ask about them? And our Sages of blessed memory said (Tanhuma) that he perceived in them an intellectual vision — that wicked men were destined to issue from them, and this is the way of derash. But it is necessary to explain the verse according to its plain sense, which is that Scripture revealed to us that Jacob's eyes were heavy from old age, so that he could not see and recognize; and given this, even though he saw two men standing before him besides Joseph — whom he recognized because he had been speaking with him, since they had announced to him, saying to him (Genesis 48:2): ״הִנֵּה בִּנְךָ יוֹסֵף״ as stated above — he did not recognize the sons. Therefore he asked about them: ״מִי אֵלֶּה?״" "<b>Further</b>, it is possible that Jacob intended to stir the father's love for the sons before blessing them, so that the blessing would be in the full force of love and affection. Therefore he asked <b>מִי אֵלֶּה</b>, so that he would hear from the mouth of his beloved son the statement ״בָּנַי הֵם״ and his innards would yearn for them — and this is the sod of (Jeremiah 31:20): ״מִדֵּי דַבְּרִי בּוֹ וְגוֹ׳ רַחֵם אֲרַחֲמֶנּוּ״ and so forth."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:50:01.057932276Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}